<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Shan State</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1948">1948</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Shan State is the largest state of Myanmar, covering the Shan Plateau in the east of the country. It was formed in 1948 from the Federated Shan States when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burma">Burma</a> became independent, after Shan leaders agreed at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/panglong-conference">Panglong Conference</a> of 1947 to join the new Union. In 1959 the hereditary saophas gave up their ruling powers to the Shan State government, ending the old principalities.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/shan-state.jpg" alt="Shan State" width="320" height="207" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Stefan Fussan · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Pindaya_Caves_0001.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Shan State borders <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> (<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yunnan">Yunnan</a>) to the north, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/laos">Laos</a> to the east, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thailand">Thailand</a> to the south, and five administrative divisions of Myanmar to the west. It covers 155,800 square kilometres, almost a quarter of the country's area, and most of it is a hilly plateau, the Shan Plateau, cut by the gorge of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/salween">Thanlwin</a> or Salween River. Inle Lake, where the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/intha">Intha</a> people live in floating villages, is the second largest natural expanse of water in the country. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taunggyi">Taunggyi</a> is the capital, and the Tai, or Shan, are the majority among the several ethnic groups of the state.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The Shan States were founded in the Shan Hills during the Pagan Kingdom, and their founding accelerated after 1287, when the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a> overthrew Pagan and the Tai who came south with them stayed. The most powerful were Mong Yang and Mong Kawng in present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kachin-state">Kachin State</a>, followed by Hsenwi, Hsipaw and Mong Mit. They were multi-ethnic, including Chin, Kachin, Wa, Pa O and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karenni-people">Kayah peoples</a>. A Mohnyin-led confederation of Shan states conquered Ava in 1527.</p><p>In 1555 King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bayinnaung">Bayinnaung</a> dislodged the Shan king from Ava and by 1557 had conquered all the Burmese Shan states, reducing them to governorships. The saophas, the rulers, kept their regalia and feudal rights, but their sons had to live in the Burmese king's palace as hostages, a policy continued until 1885. Several northern states were annexed by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qing-empire">Qing dynasty</a> in the 1730s. The Konbaung dynasty's reassertion of control led to war with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> between 1765 and 1769, and its success became the basis of the present boundary. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burma">Burma</a> lost southern Lan Na in 1776 and northern Lan Na in 1786 to Siam.</p><p>The British captured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mandalay">Mandalay</a> on 28 November 1885, but it took until 1890 to subdue all the Shan states, which then were ruled by their saophas as feudatory <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/princely-state">princely states</a>. In October 1922 the Shan and Karenni states were merged as the Federated Shan States. At the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/panglong-conference">Panglong Conference</a> in February 1947 a unitary Shan State was agreed, including the former Wa states, with the right of secession after ten years. Rebellions and the Chinese Nationalist invasion of 1950 followed independence, and in the 1960s eastern Shan State became a major opium-growing area, part of the Golden Triangle.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Shan State” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Tai Tham script spread from Chiang Mai to the Tai Lue of Sipsong Panna in Yunnan, the Tai Khun of Kengtung in the <b>Shan State</b> and Lan Xang, where it became the standard script for Buddhist texts.</blockquote><a href="/empire/lan-na">Lan Na · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>In the early 1950s Chinese Nationalist (Kuomintang) troops who had fled into <b>Shan State</b> added another war.</blockquote><a href="/empire/myanmar">Myanmar · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>At the Panglong Conference of 1947 Shan leaders agreed with Aung San to join an independent Burma, and the Federated <b>Shan States</b> became Shan State of the Union of Burma in 1948.</blockquote><a href="/state/federated-shan-states">Federated Shan States</a></li><li><blockquote>Kengtung remains the main town of eastern <b>Shan State</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/kengtung-state">Kengtung State</a></li><li><blockquote>Like other saophas, its ruler surrendered his powers to the <b>Shan State</b> government in 1959.</blockquote><a href="/state/momeik-state">Momeik State</a></li><li><blockquote>North Hsenwi became part of <b>Shan State</b> in independent Burma in 1948, and its saopha surrendered his powers in 1959.</blockquote><a href="/state/north-hsenwi">North Hsenwi</a></li></ul></section>
